share_shrink":null,"_gp_share_shrink":null,"_score_share_shrink":0.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.02021654358102956,"_gp_sbc_dilution":false,"_score_sbc_dilution":66.3,"_gate_insider_own":0.00118,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":0.8,"_gate_insider_buying":0.0,"_gp_insider_buying":false,"_score_insider_buying":0.0,"pf":[],"description_full":"Visa Inc. operates as a payments technology company in the United States and internationally. It operates VisaNet, a transaction processing network that enables authorization, clearing, and settlement of payment transactions. The company also offers credit, debit, and prepaid card products; tap to pay, tokenization, and click to pay services; Visa Direct, a platform which facilitates money movement, enabling clients to collect, hold, convert, and send funds across its network; and issuing solutions, such as airport lounge access, dining reservations, shopping experiences, event tickets, and seller offers. In addition, it provides acceptance solutions, an omnichannel payment integration with e-commerce platforms; risk detection and prevention solutions; and advisory and other services comprising consulting practice, proprietary analytics models, data scientists and economists, marketing services, and managed services. The company provides its services under the Visa, Visa Electron, V PAY, Interlink, and PLUS brands. It serves consumers, sellers, financial institutions, and government entities. Visa Inc. was founded in 1958 and is headquartered in San Francisco, California.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Ryan M. McInerney | CEO & Director | Age 50 | Compensation: $7.1M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 34K people. Revenue per employee of $1.3M ranks in the top quartile among Financial Services peers, with revenue growing 12%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $634K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $27K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 5\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (2/10).","financial_summary":["Visa Inc. is a mega-cap financial services business with a $801.5B market cap on $45.9B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +12% over the past three years and +12% annually across the full decade, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","Returns on capital run at 42% against a 9% cost of capital \u2014 a 34-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat.","The business throws off $22.1B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 48 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.01\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.3\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage of 37\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Shareholder yield of 3.2% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and within its sector the business captures 2.3\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 53% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 3 other valuation measures)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.528506735975764},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $41.7M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Credit cycles and regulatory capital \u2014 losses accumulate invisibly in good years and appear all at once, and the time to worry is when underwriting standards are loosest.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Fintech disintermediation \u2014 neobanks, embedded finance, and blockchain rails are unbundling the fee streams that traditional banks took for granted.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Interest rate regime shifts \u2014 a rapid move in rates can invert the yield curve, crush bond portfolios, and trigger deposit flight simultaneously.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Operating margins 32% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricing power","Altman Z comfortably in the safe zone \u2014 a fortress balance sheet on this measure","17 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment","ROIC sits in the top quartile of Financial Services peers \u2014 the moat is wider than the neighbourhood"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Can Visa (NYSE:V) Turn Market Optimism Into Stronger Business Growth?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/stocks/bluechip/can-visa-nysev-turn-market-optimism-into-stronger-business-growth","date":"2026-11-27","timestamp":1795796040.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.934,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Visa (NYSE:V) Puts Capital Efficiency In The Spotlight. What's Next?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/stocks/financial/visa-nysev-puts-capital-efficiency-in-the-spotlight-whats-next","date":"2026-11-27","timestamp":1795789140.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.612,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"What Is Visa (V) Doing With Stablecoin Payments In Europe And Asia?","source":"finance.yahoo.com","link":"https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/crypto/articles/visa-v-doing-stablecoin-payments-120